  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-graphify",
      "text": "> Give DeepSeek Harness a current, local map of your codebase.\n\n`dsh-graphify` connects [Graphify](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify)'s code knowledge graph to DeepSeek Harness (DSH). It helps an agent move from a question to the relevant symbols, dependencies, architectural communities, and change impact without treating a repository as an unstructured pile of files."
    },
    {
      "section": "Why use it?",
      "text": "Graphify is the local graph engine; `dsh-graphify` is the DSH-native layer that makes it practical in an agent session."
    },
    {
      "section": "Why use it?",
      "text": "| With a raw code search workflow | With dsh-graphify |\n| --- | --- |\n| The agent repeatedly guesses paths, greps, and reads broad files. | The agent can start from symbols, dependency paths, communities, and graph-backed project context. |\n| A graph can silently describe an older checkout. | Freshness checks identify stale graphs and can warn or safely run an incremental code update. |\n| Every graph capability is exposed, even when a smaller model needs only navigation. | Compact mode provides six focused tools for local 20B–30B models; full mode exposes the complete set. |\n| MCP setup and failures are separate from the DSH session. | The plugin resolves the active session project, manages the Graphify process, and provides `graphify_status` for diagnosis. |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Why use it?",
      "text": "This is not a claim that a graph replaces source reading or tests. It gives the agent a reliable starting map, so it can spend its context budget on the code that matters."
    },
    {
      "section": "Before you start",
      "text": "You need a working DSH profile, Node.js 22.18 or later, Python 3.10 or later, and [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/). The plugin is tested on macOS and Ubuntu Linux; Windows is not yet verified."
    },
    {
      "section": "1. Add the plugin to your DSH profile",
      "text": "```sh\ndsh plugin --profile <profile> add dsh-graphify\n```\n\nFor example, replace `<profile>` with the profile you normally use to run DSH. The bundle activates itself; you do not need to hand-edit a Cordis configuration for the default setup."
    },
    {
      "section": "2. Install the local Graphify runtime",
      "text": "```sh\nuv tool install 'graphifyy[mcp]==0.9.57'\n```\n\nIf Graphify is unavailable, DSH can still start. Ask for `graphify_status` after installing it; the plugin discovers the runtime on the next check or tool call, without a DSH restart."
    },
    {
      "section": "3. Build a graph for a repository",
      "text": "Start DSH from the repository you want to explore:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repository\ndsh --profile <profile>\n```\n\nThen, in the DSH conversation, run:\n\n```text\n/graphify\n```\n\nThe command performs a full build and writes `graphify-out/graph.json` in that repository. Check the result with:\n\n```text\ngraphify_status\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "4. Ask architectural questions",
      "text": "Try requests that benefit from relationships instead of filename matching:\n\n```text\nWhat are this repository's entry points and the most connected modules?\n```\n\n```text\nBefore I change this interface, show the dependency path to its callers and summarize the likely blast radius.\n```\n\n```text\nWhich community owns authentication, and where does it connect to the API layer?\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Pick a mode",
      "text": "The default `full` mode exposes the complete Graphify capability set, including communities, god nodes, and pull-request impact. Use it when your model has ample tool-selection capacity or you need those specialist queries.\n\nFor local 20B–30B models, use `compact`. It registers six high-frequency tools: status, graph query, node inspection, neighbours, shortest path, and the session-scoped graph resource. Fewer choices help smaller models use the graph consistently."
    },
    {
      "section": "Pick a mode",
      "text": "Add an override only when you need one:\n\n```yaml\n- insert:\n    - id: dsh-graphify\n      name: dsh-graphify\n      config:\n\n`freshness.mode` is `warn` by default. Choose `auto` when you want the plugin to coalesce safe incremental updates before a query; choose `off` only when you deliberately accept a static graph. A full `/graphify` build is required after documentation or other non-code changes, because incremental Graphify updates cover code extraction only."
    },
    {
      "section": "What the plugin adds",
      "text": "- **Session-aware project resolution:** graph requests follow the active DSH workspace. Model-invoked access outside that workspace is blocked by default.\n- **Freshness you can inspect:** `graphify_status` reports the resolved project, graph path, runtime availability, connection state, and whether the graph matches the indexed source state.\n- **Safe update policy:** incremental updates run only for changes the plugin can prove are code-only; otherwise it keeps the graph marked stale and asks for a full build.\n- **DSH-native operation:** `/graphify` builds or updates the receiving session's project and shows its durable result in DSH Web.\n- **Resilient local runtime:** the plugin discovers Graphify automatically and reconnects after unexpected MCP-process exits."
    },
    {
      "section": "Common tasks",
      "text": "| Goal | In DSH |\n| --- | --- |\n| Build or fully refresh the graph | `/graphify` or `/graphify build` |\n| Update changed code files | `/graphify update` |\n| Diagnose installation, project selection, or stale data | `graphify_status` |\n| Find a symbol and related architecture | Ask the agent to query the graph, then inspect the returned nodes and paths. |\n| Analyse a risky change | Ask for dependents, shortest paths, communities, or PR impact before editing. |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Common tasks",
      "text": "`/graphify update` is intentionally conservative. Run `/graphify` after changes to Markdown, package metadata, configuration, or any other non-code source that should be represented in the graph."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration reference",
      "text": "The defaults work for most single-repository sessions. These are the settings users commonly change:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration reference",
      "text": "| Setting | Default | Use it when |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `toolMode` | `full` | Set `compact` for focused navigation with smaller local models. |\n| `freshness.mode` | `warn` | Set `auto` for safe pre-query code updates, or `off` for an intentionally static graph. |\n| `graphifyVersion` | unset | Pin the Graphify version used by automatic `uv` fallback. |\n| `graphPath` | auto-detected | Point to a non-standard `graph.json` location. |\n| `cwd` | active session project | Supply a fallback project directory when a session has no workspace. |\n| `allowExternalProjects` | `false` | Allow model tools to access projects beyond the session workspace. |\n| `toolPrefix` | empty | Avoid tool-name collisions in a larger DSH profile. |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration reference",
      "text": "See [`src/config.ts`](src/config.ts) for the complete, typed configuration schema and defaults."
    },
    {
      "section": "Troubleshooting",
      "text": "| Symptom | What to do |\n| --- | --- |\n| `graphify_status` says the runtime is unavailable | Run the `uv tool install` command above, then run `graphify_status` again. |\n| No graph is found | Start DSH in the repository and run `/graphify`. |\n| The graph is stale after docs or configuration edits | Run `/graphify` for a full build. |\n| The wrong project is selected | Start DSH from the intended repository, or configure `cwd` or `graphPath`. |\n| A model needs another checkout | Keep the default isolation where possible; otherwise set `allowExternalProjects: true` deliberately. |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Compatibility and development",
      "text": "The package supports `@deepseek-ai/cordis` `>=4.0.4 <5`, is tested against DeepSeek Harness `0.2.0-rc.2` (and the `0.2.1-alpha` channel), and is tested with Graphify `0.9.57` (live contract and schema-drift checks also pass on `0.9.90`). It ships a prebuilt DSH bundle and can be developed locally with:\n\n```sh\npnpm run typecheck\npnpm run build\npnpm test\npnpm run test:drift"
    },
    {
      "section": "Compatibility and development",
      "text": "Use `GRAPHIFY_E2E=1 pnpm run test:e2e` to exercise a live local Graphify installation. Contribution guidance is in [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Evidence and next steps",
      "text": "[BENCHMARK.md](BENCHMARK.md) defines the planned, reproducible evaluation of Graphify-assisted agents against the baseline. It is an experimental protocol, not performance results. For Graphify's graph engine, supported languages, and upstream CLI documentation, visit [Graphify](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify)."
    }
  ],
